A1  - Panel Members
TI  - Scientific substantiation of a health claim related to a milk product, rich in fibre and protein, and reduction of the sense of hunger pursuant to Article 13(5) of Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006
T2  - EFSA JOURNAL
AB  - Following an application from ELVIR SAS submitted pursuant to Article 13(5) of Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006 via the Competent Authority of France, the Panel on Dietetic Products, Nutrition and Allergies was asked to deliver an opinion on the scientific substantiation of a health claim related to a milk product, rich in fibre and protein, and reduction of the sense of hunger.
   The scope of the application was proposed to fall under a health claim based on newly developed scientific evidence and including a request for the protection of proprietary data. The Panel considers that the specific protein and dietary fibre constituents proposed to exert the claimed effect and which are added to the fermented milk product are sufficiently characterised.
   The claimed effect as proposed by the applicant is reduction of the sense of hunger. The target population is the general adult population, and more specifically the female population. The claimed effect as proposed by the applicant may contribute to maintain energy balance and weight control. The Panel considers that the claimed effect might be beneficial for human health.
   The main substantiation of the claim relates to a single, randomised, double-blind and placebocontrolled study (proprietary data). In this study, 18 normal weight/slightly overweight female volunteers consumed two portions of the product daily for six weeks, compared with a parallel group of 20 volunteers consuming a placebo product. The sense of hunger after a breakfast including the product, assessed with visual analogue scales and compared with a breakfast including a placebo product, was significantly less at the beginning of the experiment but not after six weeks of consumption of two 100 mL portions of the product per day, one at breakfast and one at lunch.
   On the basis of the data presented, the Panel concludes that a cause and effect relationship has not been established between the consumption of the milk product, rich in fibre and protein, and the reduction of the sense of hunger in the female population or in the general adult population.
