TI  - <i>Trans</i>-10, <i>cis</i>-12 conjugated linoleic acid reduces milk fat content and lipogenic gene expression in the mammary gland of sows without altering litter performance
T2  - BRITISH JOURNAL OF NUTRITION
AB  - Trans-10, cis-12 conjugated linoleic acid (CLA) decreases milk fat synthesis in lactating sows and involves, at least in part, the down-regulation of lipogenic genes. The objective was to evaluate the effect of CLA on milk composition and lipogenic gene expression. Twenty multiparous sows were randomly assigned to one of the two treatments for 18 d (from day 7 to day 25 of lactation): (1) control (no CLA added) and (2) 1 % of CLA mixed into the ration. CLA treatment decreased milk fat and protein content by 20 % (P = 0 center dot 004) and 11 % (P = 0 center dot 0001), respectively. However, piglet weight did not differ between treatments (P = 0 center dot 60). Dietary CLA increased the concentration of SFA in milk fat by 16 % (P < 0 center dot 0001) and decreased MUFA by 17 center dot 6 % (P < 0 center dot 0001). In the mammary gland, CLA reduced gene expression of acetyl-CoA carboxylase-alpha by 37 % (P = 0 center dot 003), fatty acid synthase by 64 % (P = 0 center dot 002), stearoyl-CoA desaturase 1 by 52 % (P = 0 center dot 003), lipoprotein lipase by 26 % (P = 0 center dot 03), acyl glycerol phosphate acyltransferase 6 by 15 % (P = 0 center dot 02) and diacylglycerol acyltransferase 1 by 27 % (P = 0 center dot 02), whereas the expression of fatty acid binding protein 3 was not altered by CLA treatment (P = 0 center dot 09). Mammary expression of casein-beta and alpha-lactalbumin was reduced by CLA by 68 % (P = 0 center dot 0004) and 62 % (P = 0 center dot 005), respectively. Additionally, CLA had no effect on the expression of lipogenic genes evaluated in adipose tissue. In summary, CLA reduced milk fat content without negatively affecting litter performance and it affected mammary expression of genes involved in all lipogenic pathways studied.
