TI  - Weight reduction is feasible - preliminary results of a controlled, randomised intervention study in over-weight adults
T2  - ERNAHRUNGS-UMSCHAU
AB  - In the present study the effect of different lifestyle interventions, aimed at reducing body weight and body fat mass, were tested in a clinically controlled randomised design. Three different groups of 30 randomly selected persons each were formed: Group 1, dietary intervention; group 11, dietary intervention + guided physical exercise; group III, health education (counselling about health claims, diet and exercise). The trial lasted 12 months. The dietary intervention consisted of a soy and milk protein supplement (Almased(R)); the dosage of supplement intake was individually defined in consideration of body weight. As primary efficacy parameter of the intervention, body composition (body fat, lean body mass) was measured by means of the BodPod(R) technology; the BodPod(R) technology measures body composition in analogy to hydrostatic weighing, using computerised pressure sensors to determine the amount of air displaced by the person's body. In addition, body weight, BMI, WHR, waist circumference, physical performance, parameters of the metabolic syndrome, lipid metabolism and inflammatory markers were documented to objectify the therapeutic benefit of the intervention. Here, we present the results after 6 months of intervention. In all groups the intervention led to a reduction in body fat mass and a favourable modification of pro-atherogenic and pro-inflammatory risk factors. Dietary intervention reduced body weight to a higher degree (mean weight loss -8.9 kg) than lifestyle education alone (mean weight loss -6.2 kg). Physical exercise added to the dietary intervention program (group 11) did not increase weight loss. The dietary intervention as a low-fat/high-protein diet, aimed at reducing body weight by 0.5 kg/wk, was not associated with an undesirable loss in muscle mass. In conclusion, the results of the present study indicate that significant weight loss by dietary intervention with a negative energy balance in the range of 700 kcal/d is feasible and does not impair body composition in terms of a reduction in muscle mass.
