TI  - Effect of propylene glycol on fatty liver development and hepatic fructose 1,6 bisphosphatase activity in periparturient dairy cows
T2  - LIVESTOCK PRODUCTION SCIENCE
AB  - Twenty-three Holstein Friesian cows were randomly allocated to: a control group of 9 cows and a treated group of 14 cows that were drenched with 400 ml of propylene glycol once daily from 7 (6 4) days before anticipated calving date until 7 days after calving. At -2, 2, and 4 weeks from parturition, concentrations of glucose, non-esterified fatty acid, beta-hydroxylbutyrate, and total cholesterol concentrations were determined in blood samples, and concentrations of triacylglycerol. and glycogen and activity of fructose 1,6 bisphosphatase were determined in liver samples. Milk yields were recorded daily. Compared with the concentrations at -2 weeks, serum glucose concentrations were reduced at 2 and 4 weeks, but the concentrations did not differ between treatments. Serum non-esterified fatty acid concentrations did not differ between the two groups at -2 weeks; increased 269% and 118% for control and treated groups, respectively, at week 2 postpartum; and were higher at 2 and 4 weeks postpartum for control cows. Serum P-hydroxybutyrate concentration did not differ between the two groups at -2 weeks, but increased after calving and was higher for control than for treated group at 2 weeks postpartum. At all sampling times, serum cholesterol concentrations did not differ between groups, the concentrations were higher at week 4 postpartum than at weeks -2 and 2 for both groups. Liver triacylglycerol concentrations increased 245% and 125% between weeks -2 and 2 in control and treated cows, respectively. Hepatic triacylglycerol concentrations did not differ between the two groups; however, the concentrations were higher at 2 and 4 weeks for control than for treated cows. Hepatic glycogen concentrations decreased at week 2 compared to week -2, but were similar between groups at all intervals. Hepatic fructose 1,6 bisphosphatase activity did not change throughout the experimental period in both groups, and were similar in both groups at all intervals. Average 30-day milk yields were 29.1 +/- 5.3 and 30.2 +/- 4.1 kg/day for control and treated groups, respectively, and did not differ between groups. Our results indicated that dairy cows drenched with propylene glycol at a dosage of 400 ml/cow daily may reduce fat mobilization as evidenced by the reduction in plasma non-esterified fatty acids and reduce triacylglycerol accumulation in the liver. In conclusion, propylene glycol administered daily from 7 days prepartum to 7 days postpartum may reduce the risks of fatty liver and its consequences in cows. (c) 2005 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
