TI  - FATTY ACID AND POSITIONAL SPECIFICITIES OF LIPOPROTEIN LIPASE
T2  - JOURNAL OF BIOLOGICAL CHEMISTRY
AB  - Lipoprotein lipase from hen adipose tissue was incubated with chylomicrons derived from feeding rats with cannulated thoracic ducts either olive oil, corn oil, cream or cocoa butter. The molar percentage composition of the free fatty acids produced by enzymic hydrolysis was compared to the molar percentage composition of the fatty acids in the triglyceride substrate With all 4 substrates, the results clearly indicated that lipoprotein lipase does not have a fatty acid specificity among the long chain fatty acids, satu-rated or unsaturated. Controls in which the chylomicrons were similar-ly digested with pancreatic lipase demonstrated that this conclusion is not an artifact induced by non-random structure of the chylomicron tri-glycerides. Determination of the relative amounts of free fatty acids and glycerol produced during the course of hydrolysis indicated that, in contrast to pancreatic lipase, lipoprotein lipase catalyzes the hydrolysis of all 3 bonds in the triglyceride molecule at indistinguishable rates.
